Progress status for this period: stalling. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "You missed half your sessions this week, which stalled your progress — consistency is the fix.",
  "summary": "This was a tough week overall. You completed Upper A and Lower A but missed Upper B and Lower B, meaning you only got through half of your planned training. The good news is that no muscle group regressed — strength indices held steady across the board. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, you have excellent capacity to recover and build, but that potential only converts into muscle when you're hitting sessions consistently. The stall this period is a direct signal that the missed volume is catching up with you. One solid corrective week can get things moving again.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "Strength indices were completely flat across every muscle group this period. Chest held at 104, back at 103, triceps at 103, shoulders at 102, and biceps at 101 — all unchanged from last period. Legs actually slipped very slightly from 106 to 105, a one-point dip that does not meet the three-point threshold for a regression classification, but it is still a move in the wrong direction. No group rose by three or more points, so the period is correctly classified as stalling. The most likely driver is the missed Upper B and Lower B sessions — losing half your planned weekly volume means the muscles simply did not receive enough stimulus to drive adaptation forward.",
    "status": "stalling"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders account for only 9.4% of weekly volume and just 2 sets, sitting noticeably below chest (24%, 3 sets) and back (25.1%, 3 sets). For a muscle-building goal, shoulders typically benefit from a bit more direct work to keep pace with the pressing muscles around them."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps represent just 4.8% of total volume, the lowest of any group tracked. With only 2 sets and 288 kg of tonnage, direct bicep stimulus is quite limited relative to the triceps (10%, 2 sets) and the overall training load. Adding one additional set when Upper B is completed would help close this gap."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Complete all four planned sessions next week — Upper A, Lower A, Upper B, and Lower B. Missing half your training is the single biggest thing holding back your muscle-building goal right now, so protecting that schedule takes top priority.",
    "Focus on adding small load or rep increases to your main chest, back, and leg movements. Strength indices on all three have been flat for at least two consecutive periods, so even a minor progression — one extra rep or a small weight bump — will help break the stall.",
    "Give your legs a little extra attention. The leg strength index slipped from 106 to 105 this week, a small but unwanted move. Make sure your Lower A and Lower B sessions include adequate working sets and that you are not leaving reps in reserve unnecessarily.",
    "Consider adding one set each to shoulders and biceps when Upper B is completed. Both groups are on the lower end of volume relative to everything else, and bringing them up slightly will support a more balanced physique over time."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strength indices stalled across all muscle groups and legs dipped slightly; root cause was completing only Upper A and Lower A while missing Upper B and Lower B — full session adherence is the immediate priority next period."
}
